@page "/tables/advanced-table/filter/interaction"

@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Button.Enums
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.AdvancedTable.Components.Columns
@using ViciOne.Ui.Blazor.Components.Tables.Shared.Models

<h3>Filter interaction</h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    The table owns the filter it applies — no parent holds it, so no parent re-render can disturb it. These are the four
    channels that move it: a starting value it adopts once, an observation of every change, a two-way binding, and a
    call that commands a change from outside.
</DescriptionBanner>

<h3>Start with a filter — an unchanging <code>FilterState</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Pass <code>FilterState</code> from a field that is never reassigned to give the table the filter it starts
    with. The table applies it once the columns have registered and owns it from then on, because handing over
    the same instance again is not a new command. An entry for a column the table does not show is dropped.
    The filter this table starts with:
</DescriptionBanner>

<ul>
    @foreach (var activeFilter in _seedFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
    {
        <li>
            <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
            @if (activeFilter is StartsWithColumnFilter startsWithFilter)
            {
                <text> — starts with "@startsWithFilter.Value"</text>
            }
        </li>
    }
</ul>

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" FilterState="_seedFilterState" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Observe changes — <code>FilterStateChanged</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Handle <code>FilterStateChanged</code> without passing <code>FilterState</code> to watch the applied filter
    without driving it. It reports the applied filter after every change, whatever caused it — a filter applied
    here in a header, a change made from outside, or a column disappearing. The list below reports what was
    last raised.
</DescriptionBanner>

@if (_observedFilterState.Filters.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var activeFilter in _observedFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
        {
            <li>
                <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
                @if (activeFilter is StartsWithColumnFilter startsWithFilter)
                {
                    <text> — starts with "@startsWithFilter.Value"</text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>Nothing observed yet — open a column filter and apply.</em></p>
}

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider" RowClickSelectionEnabled="false" FilterStateChanged="ObservedFilterStateChanged" MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Drive the filter from markup — <code>@("@bind-FilterState")</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Bind <code>FilterState</code> to drive the table from a field and have every change written back into it,
    whoever made it — the buttons below or a column header. A new instance in the field is a command; the same
    instance again is not, which is what lets the table's own value flow back without starting over.

    <strong>Note:</strong> build the value in a field and only replace it when it really changes. A parent that
    produces a fresh, content-equal instance on every render makes the table re-apply on every render, and under
    a binding that never terminates — each apply reports back, each report renders the parent, each render
    builds another instance.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>Key filter</label>
        <Button Text="Keys starting with b" OnClick="FilterBoundTableByKey" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
    <div class="option">
        <label>All column filters</label>
        <Button Text="Clear filters" OnClick="ClearBoundFilter" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_boundFilterState.Filters.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var activeFilter in _boundFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
        {
            <li>
                <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
                @if (activeFilter is StartsWithColumnFilter startsWithFilter)
                {
                    <text> — starts with "@startsWithFilter.Value"</text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active filter.</em></p>
}

<AdvancedTable ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
               RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
               @bind-FilterState="_boundFilterState"
               MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>

<h3>Change the filter from outside — <code>SetFilterStateAsync</code></h3>

<DescriptionBanner>
    Hold the table in an <code>@("@ref")</code> and call <code>SetFilterStateAsync</code> for the changes a
    binding expresses badly — a "clear all" button, a reset to a preset — and for callers that are not in a
    render at all, such as a debounce timer or a server push. Unlike the parameter the method has no memory of
    what it was handed before, so clearing works here even though the empty filter is a value the table has
    seen before.
</DescriptionBanner>

<div class="options">
    <div class="option">
        <label>All column filters</label>
        <Button Text="Clear filters" OnClick="ClearFiltersAsync" Size="ButtonSize.Small" />
    </div>
</div>

@if (_pushedFilterState.Filters.Count > 0)
{
    <ul>
        @foreach (var activeFilter in _pushedFilterState.Filters.OfType<IColumnFilter>())
        {
            <li>
                <code>@activeFilter.ColumnId</code>
                @if (activeFilter is StartsWithColumnFilter startsWithFilter)
                {
                    <text> — starts with "@startsWithFilter.Value"</text>
                }
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
}
else
{
    <p><em>No active filter.</em></p>
}

<AdvancedTable @ref="_pushTargetTable"
               ItemsProvider="_itemsProvider"
               RowClickSelectionEnabled="false"
               FilterStateChanged="PushedFilterStateChanged"
               MaximumHeight="300px">
    <Columns>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Key" Title="Key" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Key</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Value" Title="Value" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Value</CellContent>
            <FilterEditor>
                <StartsWithColumnFilterEditor />
            </FilterEditor>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
        <AdvancedTableColumn Id="Quantity" Title="Quantity" Resizeable="false">
            <CellContent>@context.Quantity</CellContent>
        </AdvancedTableColumn>
    </Columns>
</AdvancedTable>
